{"step_index":28,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T07:45:41Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T13:15:41+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T13:15:41+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/brain/2a9dbf06-360a-4549-87fe-f576409e10a3/ECM_Chapter3_StudyGuide.md`\nTotal Lines: 169\nTotal Bytes: 14457\nShowing lines 1 to 169\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Chapter 3: Energy, Environment and Climate Change (CO-5)\n2: \n3: ## TOPIC 1: CLEAN DEVELOPMENT MECHANISM (CDM)\n4: \n5: **Q: What is CDM? Write a note on CDM methodology and its project cycle with neat sketch. Explain the objectives of CDM and how CDM promotes sustainable development. (7 marks)**\n6: \n7: CDM is one of the three flexible mechanisms defined in the Kyoto Protocol under Article 12. It allows developed countries to invest in emission reducing projects in developing countries and earn Certified Emission Reductions (CERs) or carbon credits. Each CER equals one tonne of CO2 equivalent not emitted.\n8: \n9: Objectives:\n10: 1. Help developing countries achieve sustainable development.\n11: 2. Help developed countries meet their emission reduction targets cost effectively.\n12: 3. Transfer clean technology and financial resources to developing countries.\n13: \n14: CDM Project Cycle:\n15: 1. Project Design: The project developer prepares a Project Design Document (PDD) describing the project activity, baseline methodology, monitoring plan, and expected emission reductions.\n16: 2. National Approval: The project must be approved by the Designated National Authority (DNA) of the host country. In India, this is the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change. DNA confirms the project contributes to sustainable development.\n17: 3. Validation: An independent Designated Operational Entity (DOE) accredited by the CDM Executive Board reviews the PDD and validates whether it meets CDM requirements.\n18: 4. Registration: The DOE submits the validated PDD to the CDM Executive Board for formal registration. Registration is the official acceptance of the project as a CDM project.\n19: 5. Monitoring: The project developer monitors the emission reductions as per the monitoring plan in the PDD. Monitoring data is collected throughout the crediting period.\n20: 6. Verification: A second independent DOE verifies the monitored emission reductions. This DOE must be different from the one that did validation.\n21: 7. Issuance of CERs: After successful verification, the CDM Executive Board issues CERs for the verified emission reductions. These CERs can be traded in the carbon market.\n22: \n23: [DIAGRAM NEEDED: CDM Project Cycle flowchart showing Project Design (PDD) to National Approval (DNA) to Validation (DOE) to Registration (CDM Executive Board) to Monitoring (Project Developer) to Verification (Second DOE) to CER Issuance (CDM Executive Board). Show as a vertical or circular flowchart with arrows between each step.]\n24: \n25: Examples of CDM projects in India include wind power projects, solar power, biomass power, energy efficiency improvement in industries, and methane recovery from landfills.\n26: \n27: Benefits to developing countries include technology transfer, foreign investment, employment, and sustainable development. Benefits to developed countries include cost effective emission reduction and meeting Kyoto targets.\n28: \n29: ## TOPIC 2: BACHAT LAMP YOJNA (BLY)\n30: \n31: **Q: How does the Bachat Lamp Yojna work? (3 marks)**\n32: \n33: Bachat Lamp Yojna (BLY) was launched by the Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) in 2009. It is a CDM based scheme for distributing CFL and later LED bulbs to households across India at subsidized rates. Under this scheme, BEE partners with private companies called Implementing Entities. These companies distribute CFL or LED bulbs to households by collecting old incandescent bulbs. The company recovers its cost through the sale of CERs generated by the energy savings from replacing inefficient bulbs. Households get efficient bulbs at a price of Rs 15 instead of Rs 80 to 100.\n34: \n35: **Q: Explain Bachat Lamp Yojana, its aim and benefits. (7 marks)**\n36: \n37: Aim: The aim is to reduce electricity consumption of the domestic lighting sector by replacing inefficient incandescent bulbs with energy efficient CFL or LED bulbs while using the CDM carbon credit mechanism to fund the subsidy.\n38: \n39: Scheme Design:\n40: 1. BEE acts as the coordinating agency.\n41: 2. Private Implementing Entities (IEs) are selected through competitive bidding.\n42: 3. IEs distribute CFLs or LEDs to households at highly subsidized prices.\n43: 4. Old incandescent bulbs are collected and destroyed to prevent reuse.\n44: 5. The energy savings from replacing bulbs qualify as a CDM project.\n45: 6. IEs register the project with UNFCCC and earn CERs.\n46: 7. Revenue from selling CERs in the international carbon market covers the subsidy cost.\n47: 8. Each CFL replacing a 60W incandescent bulb saves about 45W. Operating 4 hours per day saves 65 kWh per year.\n48: \n49: Benefits:\n50: 1. Reduces household electricity bills by 40 to 50 percent on lighting.\n51: 2. Reduces peak electricity demand since lighting accounts for 20 percent of India's peak demand.\n52: 3. Reduces CO2 emissions as each CFL saves about 40 kg CO2 per year.\n53: 4. Costs the government nothing since the scheme is self financing through CERs.\n54: 5. Generates employment in distribution and monitoring.\n55: 6. Promotes awareness about energy efficiency in households.\n56: 7. Reduces the need for building new power plants to meet lighting demand.\n57: \n58: The scheme originally targeted distributing 400 million CFLs across India. Later, the focus shifted from CFLs to LED bulbs under the UJALA scheme launched in 2015.\n59: \n60: ## TOPIC 3: UNFCCC\n61: \n62: **Q: Discuss the significance of UNFCCC in addressing global climate change challenges. (4 marks)**\n63: \n64: UNFCCC stands for United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change. It is an international environmental treaty adopted at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in June 1992. It came into force on 21 March 1994. Currently, 198 countries have ratified the Convention. The main objective of UNFCCC is to stabilize greenhouse gas concentrations in the atmosphere at a level that prevents dangerous human interference with the climate system. The Convention follows the principle of \"common but differentiated responsibilities\" meaning all countries share the responsibility of fighting climate change, but developed countries should take the lead since they have historically contributed more to greenhouse gas emissions.\n65: \n66: **Q: Write a short note on climate change and development of UNFCCC. (7 marks)**\n67: \n68: UNFCCC stands for United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change. It is an international environmental treaty adopted at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in June 1992. It came into force on 21 March 1994. The main objective of UNFCCC is to stabilize greenhouse gas concentrations in the atmosphere at a level that prevents dangerous human interference with the climate system.\n69: \n70: Key principles of UNFCCC:\n71: 1. Common but differentiated responsibilities: Developed countries should lead in reducing emissions.\n72: 2. Precautionary principle: Lack of full scientific certainty should not be used as a reason to delay action.\n73: 3. Right to sustainable development: Countries have the right to promote sustainable development.\n74: 4. Supportive and open international economic system: Trade measures should not be used as barriers.\n75: \n76: Classification of countries:\n77: 1. Annex I: Developed countries and economies in transition including USA, EU, Japan, Russia, and Australia.\n78: 2. Annex II: Subset of Annex I wealthy developed countries required to provide financial and technical support to developing countries.\n79: 3. Non-Annex I: Developing countries including India, China, and Brazil.\n80: \n81: Major outcomes and agreements under UNFCCC:\n82: 1. Kyoto Protocol (1997): First legally binding emission reduction targets for developed countries.\n83: 2. Copenhagen Accord (2009): Voluntary pledges.\n84: 3. Paris Agreement (2015): All countries agreed to limit warming to 2°C with Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs).\n85: \n86: UNFCCC operates through annual meetings called Conference of Parties (COP). The COP is the supreme decision making body of the Convention. UNFCCC created the framework for all subsequent climate agreements. Without UNFCCC, there would be no Kyoto Protocol, no Paris Agreement, and no CDM mechanism.\n87: \n88: ## TOPIC 4: KYOTO PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: **Q: Explain the principles and objectives of the Kyoto Protocol. Assess the effectiveness of the Kyoto Protocol in reducing greenhouse gas emissions. (4 marks)**\n91: \n92: The Kyoto Protocol is an international agreement linked to UNFCCC that was adopted in Kyoto, Japan on 11 December 1997 and came into force on 16 February 2005.\n93: \n94: Key features:\n95: 1. Set legally binding emission reduction targets for developed countries. First commitment period was 2008 to 2012 to reduce GHG emissions by an average of 5.2 percent below 1990 levels. Second commitment period was 2013 to 2020 via Doha Amendment.\n96: 2. Covers six greenhouse gases: CO2, CH4, N2O, HFCs, PFCs, SF6.\n97: 3. Has three flexible mechanisms to help countries meet targets: CDM, Joint Implementation, and Emissions Trading.\n98: 4. Developing countries including India and China have no binding targets.\n99: 5. USA never ratified the Protocol and Canada withdrew in 2012.\n100: \n101: Effectiveness: The Protocol succeeded in reducing emissions in many developed countries. The EU met their collective target. However, global emissions continued to rise because major emitters like USA, China, and India were not bound by targets. This led to the Paris Agreement in 2015 which covers all countries.\n102: \n103: **Q: Explain Sustainable development. (3 marks)**\n104: \n105: Sustainable development is defined as development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. It rests on three pillars: economic growth, social inclusion, and environmental protection. In the context of energy, sustainable development means using energy resources efficiently, switching to renewable sources, and reducing pollution and greenhouse gas emissions while maintaining economic growth.\n106: \n107: ## TOPIC 5: PROTOTYPE CARBON FUND (PCF)\n108: \n109: **Q: What is the Prototype Carbon Fund? What are its objectives? State the three primary strategic objectives of PCF. (3 marks)**\n110: \n111: The Prototype Carbon Fund (PCF) was launched by the World Bank in April 2000 with capital of US$ 180 million contributed by 6 governments and 17 private companies. PCF was the first carbon fund in the world. It was designed to demonstrate how project based greenhouse gas emission reductions under the Kyoto Protocol CDM and JI mechanisms could work in practice.\n112: \n113: Three primary objectives of PCF:\n114: 1. To show how project based transactions for greenhouse gas emission reductions can contribute to sustainable development and lower the cost of compliance with Kyoto Protocol targets.\n115: 2. To provide a learning experience for stakeholders on how the CDM and JI mechanisms work in practice.\n116: 3. To demonstrate how the World Bank can work as a financial intermediary in the carbon market, acting as trustee of the fund and purchasing emission reductions on behalf of contributors.\n117: \n118: PCF invested in 23 projects in developing countries and economies in transition covering renewable energy, energy efficiency, and waste management.\n119: \n120: ## TOPIC 6: CONFERENCE OF PARTIES (COP)\n121: \n122: **Q: State the roles and responsibilities of COP (Conference of the Parties). (4 marks)**\n123: \n124: COP stands for Conference of the Parties. It is the supreme decision making body of the UNFCCC. All countries that have ratified the Convention are represented at COP. COP meets annually in different cities around the world.\n125: \n126: Roles and responsibilities:\n127: 1. Review the implementation of the Convention and its related legal instruments.\n128: 2. Make decisions necessary to promote effective implementation of the Convention.\n129: 3. Adopt new protocols, amendments, or annexes to the Convention.\n130: 4. Review national communications and emission inventories submitted by Parties.\n131: 5. Assess the overall effects of measures taken by Parties to address climate change.\n132: 6. Mobilize financial resources for climate action in developing countries.\n133: 7. Review reports from subsidiary bodies.\n134: \n135: Notable COPs include COP 3 in Kyoto for the Kyoto Protocol, COP 15 in Copenhagen for the Copenhagen Accord, COP 21 in Paris for the Paris Agreement, and COP 26 in Glasgow for the Glasgow Climate Pact.\n136: \n137: ## TOPIC 7: CARBON CREDIT TRADE SCHEME\n138: \n139: **Q: Explain the Carbon Credit Trade Scheme. (3-4 marks)**\n140: \n141: A carbon credit is a tradable permit or certificate that allows the holder to emit one tonne of CO2 or an equivalent amount of another greenhouse gas.\n142: \n143: How the scheme works:\n144: 1. A regulatory body sets a cap on total emissions allowed.\n145: 2. Emission allowances are distributed or auctioned to industries.\n146: 3. Companies that reduce their emissions below their allowance have surplus credits.\n147: 4. Companies that emit more than their allowance must buy credits from those with surplus.\n148: 5. This creates a market based mechanism where the price of carbon is determined by supply and demand.\n149: 6. The system rewards companies that invest in clean technology and penalizes those that continue polluting.\n150: \n151: Types of carbon markets include compliance markets created by mandatory regulations and voluntary markets where companies voluntarily buy carbon credits to offset their emissions.\n152: \n153: India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS) was notified in June 2023 under the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022. BEE is the administrator. It covers obligated entities in designated sectors. India plans to have a fully operational carbon market by 2026.\n154: \n155: ## TOPIC 8: PERFORM ACHIEVE AND TRADE (PAT) SCHEME\n156: \n157: **Q: Explain the Perform Achieve and Trade (PAT) scheme. (3-4 marks)**\n158: \n159: PAT is a market based energy efficiency trading mechanism under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency. This is one of the eight missions under India's National Action Plan on Climate Change.\n160: \n161: How PAT works:\n162: 1. BEE identifies Designated Consumers (DCs) in energy intensive sectors such as thermal power plants, iron and steel, cement, aluminum, fertilizers, textile, pulp and paper, chlor alkali, railways, refineries, petrochemicals, distribution companies, and buildings.\n163: 2. Each DC is given a Specific Energy Consumption (SEC) reduction target based on their current performance.\n164: 3. DCs that reduce their SEC beyond the target earn Energy Saving Certificates (ESCerts).\n165: 4. DCs that fail to meet their target must buy ESCerts from the market or pay a penalty.\n166: 5. ESCerts are traded on two power exchanges: Indian Energy Exchange and Power Exchange India Limited.\n167: \n168: PAT Cycle I covered 478 designated consumers and achieved energy savings of 8.67 million tonnes of oil equivalent against a target of 6.686 MTOE. This exceeded the target by about 30 percent. PAT is currently in its 7th cycle, covering over 1000 designated consumers across 13 sectors.\n169: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
