ION ACT 2001\\n\\nQ: Discuss the salient features of Energy Conservation Act 2001. (7 marks)\\n\\nAnswer should cover:\\n- When enacted (October 2001, India)\\n- Objective: reduce energy intensity of Indian economy\\n- Key features (write at least 8-10 points):\\n  1. Central Government can specify norms for appliances/equipment\\n  2. Standards and labeling of equipment/appliances\\n  3. Designated consumers (large energy users) must appoint energy manager, get energy audits done, comply with norms\\n  4. Establishment of Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) under Ministry of Power\\n  5. Penalties for non-compliance\\n  6. Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC) for commercial buildings\\n  7. State Designated Agencies (SDAs) for implementation at state level\\n  8. Central Government can prohibit manufacture/sale of equipment not meeting standards\\n  9. Energy consumption standards for notified equipment\\n  10. Mandatory display of energy consumption label\\n- Notifications under the Act: government has notified list of designated consumers in 9 sectors, BEE star labeling, ECBC norms\\n- Keep it factual, no fluff\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 2 (Tier 1, asked 8/9 papers): BEE SCHEMES\\n\\nQ: Enlist the schemes of Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) and discuss any two in detail. (7 marks)\\n\\nAnswer should cover:\\nList ALL schemes:\\n1. Standards and Labeling Programme (star rating for appliances)\\n2. Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC)\\n3. Designated Consumers Programme\\n4. Demand Side Management (DSM)\\n5. Bachat Lamp Yojna (CFL/LED distribution)\\n6. SME Energy Efficiency Programme\\n7. Agriculture DSM Programme\\n8. Municipal DSM Programme\\n9. National Mission on Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE)\\n10. Perform Achieve and Trade (PAT) Scheme\\n\\nThen explain any two in detail (pick Standards & Labeling and ECBC as examples, 4-5 lines each).\\n\\nAlso write a short 3-mark version:\\nQ: Enlist various schemes of BEE and its benefits. (3 marks)\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 3 (Tier 1, asked 7/9 papers): INDIAN ENERGY SCENARIO\\n\\nQ: Discuss sector-wise energy consumption scenario in India. (4 marks)\\nAND\\nQ: Write a short note on Indian Energy Scenario. (7 marks)\\n\\nMerge into one answer (7-mark version, with note that 4-mark version = first half only).\\n\\nAnswer should cover:\\n- India is 3rd largest energy consumer globally (after China and USA)\\n- Primary energy sources: coal (~55%), oil (~30%), natural gas (~8%), hydro, nuclear, renewables\\n- Sector-wise consumption: Industry (~45%), Transport (~18%), Residential (~23%), Agriculture (~8%), Commercial (~6%)\\n- Coal dominance in power generation (~70% of electricity)\\n- Import dependency: ~80% of crude oil imported, ~50% of natural gas imported\\n- Challenges: growing population, urbanization, industrial growth increasing demand\\n- Per capita consumption still low compared to developed nations (~600 kgoe vs world average ~1800 kgoe)\\n- Government push for renewables: Solar Mission, 500 GW target by 2030\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 4 (Tier 2, asked 4/9 papers): ENERGY SECURITY\\n\\nQ: Define energy security. Enlist different strategies to achieve it. (3 marks)\\nAND\\nQ: Define energy security. Enlist strategies and discuss the role of energy conservation in achieving energy security. (7 marks)\\n\\nWrite both versions.\\n\\n3-mark answer:\\n- Definition: Energy security means continuous availability of energy in various forms, in sufficient quantities, at reasonable prices.\\n- Strategies (list 5-6): diversifying energy sources, increasing domestic production, building strategic reserves, promoting renewables, energy conservation, international cooperation\\n\\n7-mark answer: everything above plus role of energy conservation (reduces demand, reduces imports, reduces cost, reduces environmental impact)\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 5 (Tier 2, asked 4/9 papers): CLASSIFICATION OF ENERGY\\n\\nQ: Define: (i) Renewable Energy (ii) Non-Renewable Energy (iii) Per Capita Energy Consumption. (3 marks)\\nAND\\nQ: Differentiate between primary and secondary sources and give examples. (4 marks)\\n\\nWrite both answers.\\n\\nRenewable: Energy that comes from sources which are naturally replenished. Examples: solar, wind, hydro, biomass, geothermal.\\nNon-Renewable: Energy from sources that get depleted and cannot be replenished in a human timescale. Examples: coal, oil, natural gas, nuclear (uranium).\\nPer Capita Energy Consumption: Total energy consumed by a country divided by its population. India's is about 600 kgoe per year.\\n\\nPrimary sources: Found directly in nature, used without conversion. Examples: coal, crude oil, natural gas, solar radiation, wind.\\nSecondary sources: Derived from primary sources through conversion. Examples: electricity, petrol, diesel, LPG, coke.\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 6 (Tier 3 but asked 2/9, worth covering): ENERGY CONSERVATION AND ITS IMPORTANCE\\n\\nQ: Define Energy Conservation and explain its importance. (4 marks)\\n\\nDefinition: Energy conservation means reducing energy consumption by using less energy for the same work output, or getting more work from the same amount of energy.\\n\\nImportance (5-6 points):\\n1. Reduces energy bills for industries and households\\n2. Decreases dependence on imported fuels (oil, gas)\\n3. Reduces environmental pollution and greenhouse gas emissions\\n4. Extends the life of existing energy resources\\n5. Improves competitiveness of Indian industry in global market\\n6. Reduces need for new power plant construction\\n\\nGive one example: Replacing a 100W incandescent bulb with 10W LED saves 90W. For a factory with 1000 such bulbs running 10 hours/day, annual saving = 1000 x 90 x 10 x 365 / 1000 = 3,28,500 k